<!-- WHY IT EXISTS -->
<section class="wrap chapter" id="why" aria-labelledby="h-why">
  <div class="lens-intro">
    <p class="eyebrow">Why it exists</p>
    <h2 id="h-why">Make declaring cheap enough that people do it</h2>
    <div class="minilife" role="img" aria-label="Where this chapter sits in a use case's life: requested, and waiting for review"><span class="ml-line"><span class="ml-s on"><i></i>requested</span><span class="ml-s"><i></i>intake</span><span class="ml-s"><i></i>assessed</span><span class="ml-s"><i></i>reassessed</span></span><span class="ml-label">requested, and waiting for review</span></div>
    <p>Governance needs to find out what AI is in use, spot risk early, and send each use case to the right people.</p>
    <div class="why"><span class="lab">Why it matters · an invented example</span><p>At Wrenfield, one department is offered an AI feature in its student-support tool. Another wants AI in a product it's building with a partner. A third wants to add it to something it built itself. All three requests land on the same small review team.</p></div>
  </div>
  <div class="scrolly">
    <div class="steps">
      <div class="step" data-step="0" id="s-why-0"><div class="step-in">
        <p class="kicker">Many directions</p>
        <h3>Interest arrived from every direction at once</h3>
        <p>At Wrenfield, teams were being offered AI in vendor tools, wanted to use it in products built with partners, and wanted to add it to things the college built itself.</p>
      </div></div>
      <div class="step" data-step="1" id="s-why-1"><div class="step-in">
        <p class="kicker">The queue</p>
        <h3>More requests than reviewers</h3>
        <p>There was no version of a central team getting through that queue one case at a time. Interest grew faster than review capacity, which in my experience is common.</p>
      </div></div>
      <div class="step" data-step="2" id="s-why-2"><div class="step-in">
        <p class="kicker">The cost of one gate</p>
        <h3>A uniform gate filters out the small cases</h3>
        <p><span class="cl inf">inferred</span>If declaring a small, low-risk piece of work costs as much as declaring a high-risk one, the small work stops being declared, or goes around the gate. The gate then only sees the cases people are still willing to bring to it.</p>
      </div></div>
      <div class="step" data-step="3" id="s-why-3"><div class="step-in">
        <p class="kicker">The design question</p>
        <h3>Which cases need an expert at all?</h3>
        <p>The sponsor of a use case<span class="gloss" data-aud="gen"> (the person responsible for it)</span> completes the assessment themselves. Expert review happens only when their answers trigger it.</p>
        <p>It is deliberately not an IT review, so anyone responsible for a use case can answer it.</p>
        <p>Another way is expert review for every case. That can suit an organization with a handful of high-stakes use cases. It doesn't suit a queue that grows faster than the reviewers.</p>
      </div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="graphic">
      <div class="panel">
        <div class="sec dim" data-on="0,1">
          <span class="lab">Where the requests came from</span>
          <div class="flowline">
            <span class="chip">vendor tools</span><span class="chip">products built with partners</span><span class="chip">the college's own builds</span>
            <span class="arrowtxt" aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="chip ink">governance needs to see it</span>
          </div>
          <div class="note tag" data-on="1"><span class="chip flag">one review team</span><span>can't take them one case at a time</span></div>
        </div>
