      <tbody>
        <tr data-ref="s-why-2"><td><span class="cl inf">inferred</span></td><td>In my practice, a gate that costs the same for every case filters out the small ones.</td><td>My practice. Not measured.</td></tr>
        <tr data-ref="s-why-3"><td><span class="cl doc">document-supported</span></td><td>Self-completed questionnaires that compute an impact level are an established public-sector pattern.</td><td>Government of Canada, <a href="https://www.canada.ca/en/government/system/digital-government/digital-government-innovations/responsible-use-ai/algorithmic-impact-assessment.html">Algorithmic Impact Assessment tool</a>: a questionnaire whose score sets one of four impact levels.</td></tr>
        <tr data-ref="s-why-3"><td><span class="cl inf">inferred</span></td><td>In my experience, letting answers decide who needs an expert saves expert time without letting risky cases through.</td><td>My practice. Testing it needs the review decisions recorded, which is one reason I design for that.</td></tr>
        <tr data-ref="s-ans-0"><td><span class="cl">observed</span></td><td>In the panel, the answers set the review path. The submitter can't pick it.</td><td>The panel above.</td></tr>
        <tr data-ref="s-ans-1"><td><span class="cl">observed</span></td><td>An unsure answer is tried both ways, so the path becomes a range and the question is named for follow-up.</td><td>The panel above.</td></tr>
        <tr data-ref="s-ans-2"><td><span class="cl">observed</span></td><td>What the person said and what the system worked out are kept in separate columns.</td><td>The panel above.</td></tr>
        <tr data-ref="s-arch-0"><td><span class="cl inf">inferred</span></td><td>In my practice, a service's meaning lives in its decision rules, not its workflow tool.</td><td>My practice. The swap demo illustrates the argument; it doesn't test it.</td></tr>
        <tr data-ref="s-arch-1"><td><span class="cl inf">inferred</span></td><td>In my practice, one authoritative home for each kind of record has worked better than one source of truth for everything.</td><td>My practice.</td></tr>
        <tr data-ref="s-btw-0"><td><span class="cl">observed</span></td><td>Without a persistent ID, the same use case assessed twice becomes two unrelated submissions.</td><td>The reassessment demo above.</td></tr>
        <tr data-ref="s-btw-0"><td><span class="cl doc">document-supported</span></td><td>Public AI registers keep a record per tool and update it when the tool changes.</td><td>UK government, <a href="https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/guidance-for-organisations-using-the-algorithmic-transparency-recording-standard/algorithmic-transparency-recording-standard-guidance-for-public-sector-bodies">Algorithmic Transparency Recording Standard guidance</a>: records are updated and republished when substantive details change.</td></tr>
        <tr data-ref="s-btw-1"><td><span class="cl inf">inferred</span></td><td>In my practice, an intake that keeps only its recommendation can't show whether its routing works.</td><td>My practice.</td></tr>
